<h2>A quarter-century dispute over dredging</h2>
<p>The underlying contract concerned dredging the approach channel and basin for a shore-based pilot station at Sagar Island. Force majeure events delayed the work, and by letter of 19 July 2000 the Port Trust entrusted additional re-dredging necessitated by siltation and soil conditions that did not match the Trust&rsquo;s own data. The arbitrator&rsquo;s award of 3 November 2003 favoured the contractor. A Single Judge dismissed the Section 34 challenge; the Division Bench, in the Section 37 appeal, halved the amount and slashed interest to 6%, anchored on the observation that additional-work claims exceeding 70% of the original contract value of Rs. 2,86,56,200 shocked its conscience.</p>

<h2>The Supreme Court&rsquo;s correction</h2>
<p>The Court&rsquo;s reasoning proceeds in four steps. First, the internal contradiction: a court that records an award is neither perverse nor patently illegal has exhausted its grounds — &ldquo;shock&rdquo; without demonstration is conclusion without reasons. Drawing on <strong>Ssangyong</strong> (2019) and <strong>Somdatt Builders</strong> (2025), the Court held that the morality-and-justice ground requires a blatant, demonstrated conflict: <em>&ldquo;the words are not to be applied on a mere lip service.&rdquo;</em></p>
<p>Second, the completion certificate. The Division Bench had never examined the joint measurements and invoices on which the award rested. Payment for work actually done, proved by measurement, is not defeated by the employer&rsquo;s refusal to certify completion.</p>
<p>Third, severability. Partial setting aside per <strong>Gayatri Balasamy</strong> (2025) presupposes separable parts; here <em>&ldquo;the measurements and invoices raised are of one character&rdquo;</em> — an inseparable whole that could be sustained or annulled, not halved.</p>
<p>Fourth, the identity of the payer. Citing <strong>Pam Developments</strong> (2019), the Court refused any indulgent standard for government award-debtors: the State litigant takes the same Section 34/37 regime as everyone else. The operative order was unqualified: <em>&ldquo;The order impugned is set aside and the appeal of the contractor is allowed.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>What Section 37 courts may and may not do</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Permitted</strong><br>Test the Section 34 court&rsquo;s conclusion against the statutory grounds: incapacity, invalid agreement, notice defects, excess of reference, tribunal irregularity, and — for domestic awards — patent illegality or conflict with the public policy heads, each demonstrated from the award and record.</div>
  <div class="arrow">vs</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Forbidden</strong><br>Reappreciating evidence, re-pricing claims, trimming quantum or interest to a figure the court finds more comfortable, or invoking &ldquo;morality and justice&rdquo; as a label for disagreement with the arbitrator&rsquo;s arithmetic.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">The judgment continues the Supreme Court&rsquo;s steady tightening of award-review discipline: finality attaches at the Section 37 stage, and quantum discomfort — even where the paying party is public — is not a legal ground. Parties drafting challenges should plead the recognised grounds with record-based demonstration, because conclusory invocations now fail predictably.</div>
